    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=089b582f-0a35-44db-98f2-170a98083ab9&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9204898179924_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Instructional Designer and AI Learning Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\n\nLocation: Trenton\n\nInstructional Designer and AI Learning Specialist U24:   $76,486.40 - $122,359.00 (Salaries for internal candidates are determined by a promotional formula. Salaries for external candidates are determined up to step 4: $87,954.55, or dependent on qualifications)  Learning Technology, Innovation, and Services    102 West State Street Trenton, NJ 08608 Thomas Edison State University was founded in 1972 to break down barriers to degree completion. Fifty years later, it proudly serves a diverse body of adult students with innovative and professionally relevant academic programming in a predominantly online delivery. Our team of high-energy student-focused professionals is welcoming new members who are making their mark in education and are ready to help change the lives of thousands of adult students across New Jersey and around the world.   Candidates who are excited by changing lives, committed to the success of all and want to be challenged by new and innovative opportunities, should submit an application.   Summary: The Instructional Designer & AI Learning Specialist designs, develops, and revises online courses in the Canvas learning management system using Thomas Edison State University's ADDIE-based course development model. Working closely with subject matter experts, assessment developers, learning technologists, and academic leadership, the Instructional Designer & AI Learning Specialist translates disciplinary expertise into pedagogically sound learning experiences that align objectives, activities, and assessments and meet institutional standards for accessibility, course mapping, and credit hour alignment. This role also leads the responsible integration of artificial intelligence into course design and development — building prompts, templates, and AI-supported workflows that accelerate production, embedding AI-enhanced learning activities tied to workforce-relevant skills, and guiding teams in applying institutional AI policies and academic integrity expectations. In addition, the Instructional Designer & AI Learning Specialist manages course revision projects from intake through completion, balancing multiple competing priorities against fixed academic deadlines such as term starts and program rollouts, and brings a deep understanding of the self-directed, practical needs of adult learners in digital environments. Job Duties and Responsibilities:   Instructional Design - Design, build, revise, and implement online courses in Canvas consistent with TESU's ADDIE-based course development model, ensuring alignment among learning objectives, activities, and assessments. - Evaluate and select instructional materials — including OER, multimedia, and online resources — that support effective and sustainable course design. - Guide subject matter experts in developing content and integrating it into sound learning experiences, applying instructional design theory, UX principles, learning technologies (e.g., LTI integrations), and emerging tools. - Review and edit course content for clarity, quality, and adherence to instructional design standards and c copyright guidelines. - Ensure courses meet institutional quality standards, including ADA accessibility, course mapping, credit hour alignment, and student success and engagement priorities. - Collaborate with staff, faculty, SMEs, and other stakeholders using Canvas, Google Docs, MS Teams, and Slack - Observe cybersecurity best practices.   AI-Assisted Course Design and Development - Guide course design and development teams in applying institutional AI policies, promoting ethical AI use, and supporting academic integrity through clear expectations and responsible practices. - Lead the incorporation of AI-infused course learning activities (case studies, projects, written assignments, etc.) that are aligned with workforce-relevant skills and program outcomes. - Collaborate with the Learning Technologist and Artificial Intelligence Coordinator to apply AI coding assistants, lightweight scripting, and automation tools to streamline repetitive instructional design workflows and accelerate creation of syllabi, learning objectives, evaluation rubrics, and accessible course assets. - Continuously upskill the instructional design team’s AI integration skills by developing prompts, templates, AI agents, and generative AI materials that accelerate creation of syllabi, learning objectives, active learning scenarios, case studies, and accessible course assets. - Support AI tool integration in alignment with TESU strategic priorities, data governance, privacy, security, compliance, and bias mitigation, and monitor emerging AI trends and research to inform course design and institutional adoption.   Project Management - Partner with the Senior Director on the course project intake process and with Associate and Assistant Deans on prioritizing and coordinating course revision projects. - Manage multiple competing course project priorities, navigating academic governance, and consistently meeting tight, fixed deadlines (e.g., term starts) without compromising course quality or student experience. - Independently organize, drive, and execute complex instructional design initiatives from inception through completion in a fast-paced higher education environment. - Participate in university initiatives, committees, and assigned projects.   Additional Competencies - Deep understanding of the unique motivations, self-directed nature, and practical needs of adult learners in an online learning environment.  - Strong interpersonal skills to build trusting relationships across instructional design, assessment development, learning technology, and academic leadership teams.    Requirements: Education: Graduation from an accredited college with a Bachelor's degree supplemented by a Master's degree in a field related to the position to be filled or an equivalency as determined by the appointing authority.    Experience: Minimum of three (3) years of professional instructional design or related experience in a higher education institution directly related to the functions of the position to be filled (e.g., Instructional Designer), or equivalency as determined by the appointing authority.  - Applicants who do not possess the required education may substitute required experience on a year-for-year basis (30 credit hours is considered one year of college). - A Ph.D. or an Ed.D. degree may be substituted for two years of the required experience.    *TESU strives to offer a flexible work environment while balancing the operational needs of the University. As such, we have several options for hybrid-work schedules. Positions determine what type of hybrid-work schedule is available and, if necessary, adjustments are made to support University operations.    Thomas Edison State University is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=089b582f-0a35-44db-98f2-170a98083ab9&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9204898167212_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Learning Technologist and AI Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\n\nLocation: Trenton\n\nLearning Technologist  and AI Coordinator U24: $76,486.40 - $122,359.00 (Salaries for internal candidates are determined by a promotional formula. Salaries for external candidates are determined up to step 4: $87,954.55, or dependent on qualifications)  Learning Technology, Innovation, and Services  102 West State Street Trenton, NJ 08608   Thomas Edison State University was founded in 1972 to break down barriers to degree completion. Fifty years later, it proudly serves a diverse body of adult students with innovative and professionally relevant academic programming in a predominantly online delivery. Our team of high-energy student-focused professionals is welcoming new members who are making their mark in education and are ready to help change the lives of thousands of adult students across New Jersey and around the world. Candidates who are excited by changing lives, committed to the success of all and want to be challenged by new and innovative opportunities, should submit an application.   Summary:   The Learning Technologist and Artificial Intelligence Coordinator will play a key role in shaping engaging, accessible, and future-ready online learning experiences. This position brings together Canvas LMS expertise, instructional technology support, and the responsible use of generative AI to help course design and development teams work more efficiently and create stronger learning experiences for adult learners. Working in partnership with technology teams and academic leadership, this role will support high-quality course delivery, streamline digital learning workflows, and help guide the ethical, secure, and academically responsible use of AI across instructional and operational practices.   AI-Assisted Course Design and Development - AI-Assisted Course Design and Development: Collaborate with instructional designers, assessment developers, and subject matter experts to develop prompts, templates, AI agents, and generative AI-supported materials that accelerate the creation of syllabi, learning objectives, active learning scenarios, case studies, and accessible course assets. - Workflow Automation and Operational Efficiency: Use AI coding assistants, lightweight scripting, and automation tools to streamline repetitive instructional design, assessment, and administrative tasks across projects. - Responsible AI Adoption and Governance: Support the integration of AI tools into institutional operations and course development in alignment with TESU strategic priorities, data governance policies, privacy, security, compliance, bias mitigation, and responsible-use expectations. - AI Ethics and Academic Integrity: Guide course design and development teams in applying institutional AI policies, promoting ethical AI use, and supporting academic integrity through clear expectations, responsible practices, and appropriate use of AI tools. - Research and Continuous Improvement: Monitor emerging AI trends, research, and best practices in online learning to inform course design, operational improvements, and institutional AI adoption.   Canvas Operations & Support - Canvas LMS Proficiency: Demonstrated ability to configure and maintain course spaces, assist users with basic system administration and troubleshooting, and manage common third-party tool integrations (LTIs). - Support Canvas administrative operations, including feature integrations, LTI tools, permission structures, and troubleshooting for third-party software.  - Implement Course Revisions & New Builds: Partner with Instructional Designers to build, configure, and launch completed course revisions and new course developments within Canvas, ensuring precise structural setup, tool alignment, and adherence to institutional design standards. - Resolve Technical Issues & Provide User Support: Serve as a primary point of contact for routine Canvas system administration, providing timely troubleshooting, issue resolution, and hands-on guidance for staff. - Build AI-Ready Knowledge Base & Technical Documentation: Author, maintain, and structure comprehensive knowledge articles and process guides for learning technologies, optimizing content specifically for integration with Zendesk AI tools to drive self-service resolution and streamline user support. - Champion Universal Design for Learning (UDL) principles and web accessibility standards (WCAG 2.2) across all digital course builds. Additional Competencies: - Consultative Guidance & Advisory: Ability to translate technical concepts into clear, encouraging language for instructors with varying levels of tech proficiency. - Adult Learning Focus: Deep understanding of the unique motivations, self-directed nature, and practical needs of adult learners in digital spaces. - Agility & Continuous Curiosity: A proactive drive to stay ahead of EdTech and AI developments, continuously testing, evaluating, and adapting emerging technologies. - Cross-Functional Collaboration: Strong interpersonal skills to build trusting relationships across instructional design teams, IT departments, and academic leadership. - Project Management & Operational Agility: Demonstrates the ability to independently organize, drive, and execute complex learning technology initiatives from inception through completion within a fast-paced higher education environment. Adept at managing multiple competing priorities, navigating academic governance, and consistently meeting tight, fixed deadlines (e.g., term starts, platform rollouts, staff training cycles) without compromising quality or user experience.    Requirements: Education: Graduation from an accredited college with a Bachelor's degree supplemented by a Master's degree in a field related to the position to be filled or an equivalency as determined by the appointing authority.    Experience: Three years professional experience in a field that is directly related to the functions of the position to be filled or equivalency as determined by the appointing authority    - Applicants who do not possess the required education may substitute required experience on a year-for-year basis (30 credit hours is considered one year of college). - A Ph.D. or an Ed.D. degree may be substituted for two years of the required experience.  Preferred Requirements:  - Canvas LMS Proficiency: Demonstrated ability to configure and maintain course spaces, assist users with basic system administration and troubleshooting, and manage common third-party tool integrations (LTIs). - Higher Education Track Record: Proven experience working in a Higher Education setting, specifically supporting online course development and adult learning programs.  - Hands-On AI Experience: Demonstrated background using Generative AI tools (e.g., ChatGPT, Claude, Midjourney, Copilot) within instructional design or educational context workflows. - Master’s degree in educational technology, or a closely related field. - Core LMS Expertise: Advanced to expert-level experience in Canvas LMS administration, course architecture, LTI integrations, and technical troubleshooting. - AI Policy: Experience contributing to institutional AI governance, academic integrity guidelines, or AI pilot program evaluations. - Experience using agentic AI coding tools (such as ChatGPT Codex, Claude Code) to build lightweight automations, scripts, or internal utilities that support teaching, learning, or academic operations systems. - Understanding of how API-based integrations (e.g., LMS, SIS, or ERP systems such as Ellucian Colleague) and emerging AI integration standards (such as MCP) support automation and AI-enabled workflows in an academic environment *TESU strives to offer a flexible work environment while balancing the operational needs of the University. As such, we have several options for hybrid-work schedules. Positions determine what type of hybrid-work schedule is available and, if necessary, adjustments are made to support University operations.  Thomas Edison State University is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=089b582f-0a35-44db-98f2-170a98083ab9&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9205017084400_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Director, Digital Experience and Engagement",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\n\nLocation: Trenton\n\nDirector, Digital Experience and Engagement D30: $130,000 – $135,000 Learning, Innovation, and Technology Solutions (LITS) 102 West State Street Trenton, NJ 08608  Thomas Edison State University was founded in 1972 to break down barriers to degree completion. Fifty years later, it proudly serves a diverse body of adult students with innovative and professionally relevant academic programming in a predominantly online delivery. Our team of high-energy student-focused professionals is welcoming new members who are making their mark in education and are ready to help change the lives of thousands of adult students across New Jersey and around the world. Candidates who are excited by changing lives, committed to the success of all and want to be challenged by new and innovative opportunities, should submit an application.   Summary: The Director, Digital Experience and Engagement provides strategic and operational leadership for the University’s digital engagement ecosystem. The director is accountable for the portfolio of platforms that enable coordinated, accessible, secure, and audience-centered interaction across recruitment, enrollment, learning, student support, employee services, completion, alumni engagement, and other University relationships. The University’s customer relationship management system is the role’s immediate anchor platform, but not its organizational boundary. The portfolio is expected to include the student portal and may expand to enterprise email and text messaging, notifications, chat and conversational services, knowledge tools, appointment and form technologies, communication preference management, and responsible AI-enabled engagement capabilities. The director works primarily with University departments and service owners to translate institutional goals, audience needs, and business processes into a coherent technology roadmap. The role convenes partners across LITS, Admissions, University Communications, Student Success, Academic Affairs, schools, and other units. It owns enabling platforms, channel architecture, technical governance, product roadmaps, and service outcomes; it does not replace the institutional voice, brand, editorial, or campaign ownership of University Communications and functional offices.   Leadership Scope:   - Establish Digital Experience and Engagement as a collaborative, enterprise-facing function rather than a single-product administration team. - Serve as LITS product and portfolio owner for assigned engagement platforms, with authority to recommend standards, consolidation, investment, lifecycle decisions, and operating models. - Develop internal platform expertise during the Element451 implementation in partnership with the named University implementation lead and prepare LITS for sustainable technical stewardship after implementation. - Provide enough technical depth to guide architecture, integration, configuration, privacy, security, and vendor decisions while coordinating hands-on engineering with the appropriate LITS teams and vendors. - Build a high-trust operating model in which ownership is clear, delivery is shared, and cross-functional partners are engaged early. Essential Responsibility Areas:   Portfolio strategy and governance - Develop and maintain a University-wide roadmap for assigned digital experience and engagement capabilities, linking investment decisions to institutional priorities and audience outcomes. - Inventory and rationalize engagement platforms; clarify the intended purpose, audience, lifecycle, owner, and integration pattern for each channel and product. - Establish standards for platform selection, configuration, channel use, permissions, accessibility, privacy, records, communication preferences, automation, AI, and lifecycle management. - Convene a cross-functional governance structure that defines decision rights without displacing the content, policy, or business-process authority of partner offices. CRM and engagement platform stewardship - Partner with the University’s named Element451 implementation lead to build LITS platform knowledge, document the operating model, and prepare for long-term technical stewardship. - Own assigned platform roadmaps, configuration standards, release planning, administrative practices, service levels, vendor relationships, and support escalation models. - Translate functional needs into prioritized platform requirements and coordinate integration, identity, security, data, and infrastructure work with Enterprise Business Systems, Enterprise Technologies, Data & Analytics, and vendors. - Ensure that roles, environments, documentation, testing, change controls, recovery plans, and knowledge transfer support reliable operations. Digital experience and portal leadership - Lead the authenticated portal as an enterprise digital front door, with clear product ownership, roadmap, governance, and measures of usefulness. - Organize experiences around audience needs, tasks, and lifecycle moments rather than University organizational boundaries. - Coordinate content, alerts, transactions, search, personalization, accessibility, and integration with University Communications, functional offices, web leadership, and LITS platform owners. - Use research, service data, and feedback to identify friction and prioritize improvements across student, employee, mentor/faculty, and other University experiences. Cross-functional service and journey enablement - Help departments map audience journeys, communication needs, interventions, and handoffs, and then apply enterprise platforms to support them. - Facilitate shared design decisions among Admissions, University Communications, schools, student services, academic and administrative offices, and LITS teams. - Define escalation paths and coordination practices for urgent, sensitive, high-impact, or cross-channel communications. - Promote reusable patterns, templates, and communities of practice so departments can collaborate freely while maintaining enterprise standards. Platform operations, vendors, and resources - Develop annual plans, budgets, staffing recommendations, contract strategies, service expectations, and lifecycle plans for the portfolio. - Lead assigned staff and coordinate matrixed project teams, consultants, and vendors; set clear priorities and follow-through expectations. - Manage platform risks, incidents, maintenance, adoption, technical debt, and vendor performance in collaboration with security, infrastructure, application, data, and service teams. - Maintain decision records, platform documentation, ownership maps, and practical support knowledge. Measurement, adoption, and continuous improvement  - Define outcomes and measures for reach, engagement, task completion, service adoption, satisfaction, reliability, accessibility, and platform value. - Work with Data & Analytics and functional partners to create appropriate dashboards and insight loops without duplicating institutional analytics ownership. - Lead adoption and change-enablement efforts for new or improved platforms, including communication, training, documentation, and stakeholder readiness. - Use evidence to prioritize improvements, retire low-value tools, and reduce fragmented or contradictory digital experiences. People and organizational leadership - Recruit, develop, coach, and evaluate staff with strengths in platform administration, business analysis, digital product ownership, workflow, communication technology, and service design. - Create role clarity and development paths for staff transitioning from legacy application responsibilities into modern engagement-platform work. - Model collaborative, non-territorial leadership and represent Digital Experience and Engagement on the LITS leadership team and University committees. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities:   - Strategic product and portfolio leadership across CRM, portals, digital communication, service, or related enterprise platforms. - Strong technical fluency in SaaS administration, configuration, integrations, APIs, identity, data flows, privacy, security, accessibility, release management, and service operations - sufficient to direct specialists and make sound tradeoffs. - Ability to translate institutional goals and audience needs into roadmaps, governance, requirements, operating models, and measurable outcomes. - Demonstrated facilitation, influence, and conflict-navigation skills in a matrixed environment with shared ownership. - Knowledge of higher-education student and employee lifecycles, digital accessibility, data privacy, responsible AI, and change management. - Excellent written, verbal, presentation, vendor-management, and staff-development skills. Requirements:   - Bachelor’s degree in information systems, business, communications technology, digital experience, higher-education administration, or a related field; an equivalent combination of education and relevant experience may be considered. - Seven years of progressively responsible experience with enterprise platforms, digital products, CRM, portals, technology-enabled service delivery, business analysis, or a closely related area. - Three years of leadership experience involving staff, vendors, major projects, cross-functional governance, or enterprise product ownership. - Demonstrated experience leading complex platform implementation, adoption, governance, or continuous improvement across organizational boundaries. Preferred Qualifications: - Master’s degree in a relevant discipline. - Experience in higher education and familiarity with CRM, student information/ERP, learning management, portal, identity, service management, and analytics ecosystems. - Experience with Element451 or a comparable higher-education CRM; digital experience platforms; journey orchestration; communication automation; knowledge or conversational AI technologies. - Experience building a new organizational capability or redirecting legacy roles toward a future-state operating model.  *TESU strives to offer a flexible work environment while balancing the operational needs of the University. As such, we have several options for hybrid-work schedules. Positions determine what type of hybrid-work schedule is available and, if necessary, adjustments are made to support University operations.   Thomas Edison State University is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer",
      "department": ""
    }
  },
